-- title:
--   Every nonnegative q-coefficient of j is at least 1
-- statement:
--   Let $n$ be a natural number. The assertion is that the coefficient of the $q$-expansion `jq` at the integer index $(n : \mathbb{Z})$ is at least $1$ as a rational number, i.e. $1 \le \mathrm{coeff}_{n}(\mathtt{jq})$. Here `jq` is the formal $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-invariant as set up in the project, a series with rational coefficients indexed by $\mathbb{Z}$, whose coefficients at the indices $0, 1, 2, \dots$ are the classical values $744, 196884, 21493760, \dots$; the statement ranges only over nonnegative indices, so it says nothing about the coefficient at $-1$ (the leading $q^{-1}$ term) or at other negative indices, and it records only the inequality $\ge 1$ rather than integrality or the exact values. In particular every coefficient of `jq` in nonnegative degree is nonzero and positive.
--
--   This positivity bound replaces the classical fact that the $q$-expansion $j(q) = q^{-1} + 744 + 196884q + \cdots$ has positive integral coefficients, in the weak form needed downstream. It is used to separate $j(q^{\ell})$ from the subfield generated by $j(q)$, and thence in the irreducibility and splitting statements for the modular polynomial $\Phi_\ell$ ([`ModularCurve.PhiGen.evalSymm_of_splits`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PhiGen.evalSymm_of_splits), [`ModularCurve.PhiGen.phiIrreducible_of_splits`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PhiGen.phiIrreducible_of_splits), [`ModularCurve.jqNModC_prime_not_mem_adjoin_of_charZero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.jqNModC_prime_not_mem_adjoin_of_charZero)).
